#! /usr/bin/env ruby
# coding: utf-8

# QSS - Quick SSM Session
# https://github.com/bogbert/qss
#
# This script helps manage and connect to EC2 instances via AWS Systems Manager (SSM).
# It's designed for users working with multiple environments spread across several
# AWS accounts and regions. QSS maintains a local database of instances, indexed by
# account, environment, and short name for quick access.
# QSS leverages bash completion to streamline command entry, displaying available options
# for accounts, environments, and instance names.
#
# Main features:
#   - Connect to EC2 instances via SSM with a simple command line interface
#   - Support bash completion for command-line arguments
#   - Update the local instance database (option --refresh)
#   - List instances from the database (option --list)
#   - Display detailed information about instances (option --info)
#
# Prerequisites:
#   - Ruby 2.4 or higher
#     A Ruby version supporting recent releases of the 'aws-sdk-ec2' gem is recommended
#     for compatibility and security
#   - Ruby gems:
#     - aws-sdk-ec2 (for EC2 instance interactions)
#     - json-schema (for configuration validation)
#   - AWS CLI installed and configured
#   - Session Manager plugin for AWS CLI
#   - AWS API access with permissions to list EC2 instances and establish SSM sessions
#
# Configuration:
#   - Managed via a JSON file (default: ~/.qss.json)
#   - Instance database stored in a JSON file (default: ~/.qss.db)
#
# Conventions:
#   - Account names, environment names, and instance short names are expected to be in lowercase
#   - Naming templates support automatic extraction of environments and short names from instance names
#
# Usage examples:
#   qss dev staging app-server      # Connect to the specified instance via SSM
#   qss --refresh                   # Refresh the entire database
#   qss --refresh dev               # Refresh only the 'dev' account
#   qss --list dev                  # List instances in the 'dev' account
#   qss --info dev staging          # Show detailed info about 'dev/staging' instances

